1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,500
It's May 2010. A sixteen-year-old
named Kalief Browder is taken to

2
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:10,980
Rikers Island, New York City's main
jail, to wait for his trial.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,330
He will wait about
eleven hundred days.

4
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:16,310
The trial will never come.

5
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:20,430
He was accused of robbing a
man of his backpack.

6
00:00:21,060 --> 00:00:22,770
He said he hadn't done it.

7
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:27,500
The case against him rested on one man's
memory, and that man's account

8
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:29,550
of when it happened had changed.

9
00:00:31,830 --> 00:00:35,610
For the first ten weeks, he was held
because his family couldn't pay

10
00:00:35,630 --> 00:00:38,050
three thousand dollars bail.

11
00:00:38,070 --> 00:00:41,850
After that, because he had been on
probation, a judge held him without

12
00:00:41,870 --> 00:00:46,100
bail. From then on, no amount of
money would have freed him.

13
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:51,870
Again and again, he was offered a
deal if he pleaded guilty.

14
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:57,170
The last, in 2013, would have let
him go home that same day.

15
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:00,610
He said no. He said he hadn't done it.

16
00:01:02,110 --> 00:01:04,440
Then the case fell apart.

17
00:01:05,470 --> 00:01:09,440
The man who had accused him had gone
back to Mexico, and without him,

18
00:01:09,790 --> 00:01:11,740
prosecutors couldn't go to trial.

19
00:01:12,630 --> 00:01:16,670
Kalief Browder went into Rikers at
sixteen, and spent much of his

20
00:01:16,690 --> 00:01:19,780
time there, by his own account,
in solitary confinement.

21
00:01:20,830 --> 00:01:23,740
He came out at twenty, never
convicted of anything.

22
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:27,650
His case was extreme.

23
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:29,080
Waiting in jail isn't.

24
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:35,540
In mid-2024, about four hundred and
fifty thousand people in American

25
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:37,640
jails hadn't been convicted.

26
00:01:37,660 --> 00:01:39,710
That's two in every three
people inside.

27
00:01:42,270 --> 00:01:45,340
So, should anyone be jailed for
years before a trial?

28
00:01:45,690 --> 00:01:48,360
Should it ever come down to
whether they can pay?

29
00:01:49,030 --> 00:01:54,000
And if someone has to be held, who
should decide, and for how long?

30
00:01:56,440 --> 00:02:00,340
We went through the reporting on his
case, the federal investigation

31
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:05,060
of Rikers, the Supreme Court's rulings,
New York's bail and speedy-trial

32
00:02:05,080 --> 00:02:09,610
laws and what happened when they
changed, and how England does it.

33
00:02:10,140 --> 00:02:14,430
At the end, we'll give you a verdict,
and tell you how sure we are.

34
00:02:27,042 --> 00:02:30,442
Kalief Browder grew up in the Bronx,
near the zoo, the youngest of

35
00:02:30,462 --> 00:02:35,142
seven children. His mother took in
dozens of foster children as well.

36
00:02:35,162 --> 00:02:38,902
In the spring of 2010, he was a
high school sophomore.

37
00:02:38,922 --> 00:02:42,052
Most of what we know about what happened
next comes from his own account,

38
00:02:42,522 --> 00:02:45,562
and from the reporter Jennifer
Gonnerman, who read the court records

39
00:02:45,582 --> 00:02:47,192
and interviewed the people involved.

40
00:02:48,022 --> 00:02:49,612
His court file is now sealed.

41
00:02:51,252 --> 00:02:54,772
Early on Saturday, May the fifteenth,
he and a friend were walking

42
00:02:54,792 --> 00:02:57,022
home from a party when
police stopped them.

43
00:02:57,412 --> 00:03:00,402
An officer said a man had just
reported that they'd robbed him.

44
00:03:00,872 --> 00:03:02,902
The police searched him
and found nothing.

45
00:03:03,432 --> 00:03:07,072
As Browder remembers it, the man then
said the robbery hadn't happened

46
00:03:07,092 --> 00:03:09,002
that night, but two weeks earlier.

47
00:03:11,462 --> 00:03:15,182
He was already on probation, for an
earlier case that had left him

48
00:03:15,202 --> 00:03:16,372
no criminal record.

49
00:03:17,002 --> 00:03:18,892
That would matter more
than anything else.

50
00:03:21,412 --> 00:03:24,252
The complaint said the two had chased
the man, pushed him against

51
00:03:24,272 --> 00:03:30,842
a fence and taken his backpack, with a
camera, an iPod and seven hundred

52
00:03:31,892 --> 00:03:34,392
dollars inside, and that
Browder had punched him.

53
00:03:34,412 --> 00:03:39,372
He was charged with robbery,
grand larceny and assault.

54
00:03:39,392 --> 00:03:43,082
Seventeen hours after the stop, questioned
by an officer and a prosecutor,

55
00:03:43,612 --> 00:03:45,202
he said again that he was innocent.

56
00:03:47,962 --> 00:03:50,032
The next day, his friend was released.

57
00:03:50,762 --> 00:03:54,442
Because Browder was on probation, the
judge set bail at three thousand

58
00:03:54,462 --> 00:03:57,122
dollars. His family couldn't raise it.

59
00:03:57,142 --> 00:03:59,332
He was taken to Rikers Island.

60
00:04:01,472 --> 00:04:06,352
A prosecutor would later call it, in court
papers, a relatively straightforward

61
00:04:06,372 --> 00:04:09,742
case. There were no
wiretaps to go through.

62
00:04:10,432 --> 00:04:12,562
There was the memory of one man.

63
00:04:14,922 --> 00:04:19,342
Ten weeks later, on July the
twenty-eighth, he was indicted for robbery

64
00:04:19,362 --> 00:04:22,232
in the second degree, and
pleaded not guilty.

65
00:04:22,902 --> 00:04:26,452
By then, it no longer mattered whether
his family could find the money.

66
00:04:27,082 --> 00:04:30,842
As is standard when someone on probation
is charged with a new violent

67
00:04:30,862 --> 00:04:35,142
felony, the probation department had
filed a violation, and the judge

68
00:04:35,162 --> 00:04:37,112
had ordered him held without bail.

69
00:04:37,802 --> 00:04:40,292
His lawyer asked for bail
again and again.

70
00:04:41,022 --> 00:04:44,172
Each time, the probation
violation stood in the way.

71
00:04:46,952 --> 00:04:49,102
So, the first weight on the scale.

72
00:04:50,092 --> 00:04:52,132
A sixteen-year-old held on
one man's account.

73
00:04:52,152 --> 00:04:54,372
Money put him in jail.

74
00:04:54,392 --> 00:04:58,942
After ten weeks, money had
nothing to do with it.

75
00:05:05,010 --> 00:05:07,350
His case went to the courts of the
Bronx, which were overwhelmed.

76
00:05:07,370 --> 00:05:12,190
In 2011, nearly three quarters of the
borough's felony cases were

77
00:05:12,210 --> 00:05:13,230
more than six months old.

78
00:05:13,250 --> 00:05:19,350
That year, a hundred and sixty-five
felony cases went to trial.

79
00:05:19,370 --> 00:05:22,140
In almost four thousand, the
defendant pleaded guilty.

80
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:26,090
New York has a rule meant to
prevent long waits.

81
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:30,110
For a felony, prosecutors have six
months to be ready for trial.

82
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:34,530
If they aren't, and the defense asks,
the charges must be dismissed.

83
00:05:35,280 --> 00:05:39,250
Soon after the indictment, prosecutors
told the court they were ready.

84
00:05:41,350 --> 00:05:44,830
But once prosecutors have said they're
ready, delays caused by a crowded

85
00:05:44,850 --> 00:05:46,370
court don't count against them.

86
00:05:46,390 --> 00:05:52,750
On January the twenty-eighth, 2011, his
two hundred and fifty-eighth

87
00:05:52,770 --> 00:05:56,750
day, a prosecutor told the court: the
People are not ready, we are

88
00:05:56,770 --> 00:05:58,690
requesting one week.

89
00:05:58,710 --> 00:06:01,320
The next date the court could
offer was six weeks away.

90
00:06:01,910 --> 00:06:03,720
Only one of those weeks counted.

91
00:06:05,980 --> 00:06:10,440
The reasons given in court: the
prosecutor was on another trial, or

92
00:06:10,460 --> 00:06:14,370
on jury duty, or had a scheduling
conflict, or was on vacation.

93
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:17,750
Month after month, the
case was put off.

94
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:21,490
By the end of 2012, it had been
before eight judges.

95
00:06:23,150 --> 00:06:24,020
Who was responsible?

96
00:06:25,130 --> 00:06:29,650
The Bronx district attorney, Robert
Johnson, said in 2014 that delays

97
00:06:29,670 --> 00:06:32,970
like these were horrendous, but that his
office wasn't really accountable

98
00:06:32,990 --> 00:06:37,050
for them. A lawsuit later filed for
Browder alleged that prosecutors

99
00:06:37,070 --> 00:06:39,220
said they were ready when
they never were.

100
00:06:39,850 --> 00:06:43,630
The court records are sealed, and that
lawsuit was settled, so neither

101
00:06:43,650 --> 00:06:45,180
claim has been tested.

102
00:06:47,740 --> 00:06:49,390
Meanwhile, the offers came.

103
00:06:50,260 --> 00:06:54,170
In early 2012, three and a half years
in prison if he pleaded guilty,

104
00:06:54,860 --> 00:06:57,830
against up to fifteen if he
went to trial and lost.

105
00:06:58,780 --> 00:07:02,590
He said no. Later that year,
two and a half years.

106
00:07:02,980 --> 00:07:08,560
He said no again. His lawyer, Brendan
O'Meara, said ninety-nine out

107
00:07:08,580 --> 00:07:12,030
of a hundred would take the offer
that gets you out of jail.

108
00:07:14,550 --> 00:07:18,880
In March 2013, a judge sent from
Brooklyn to clear the Bronx backlog,

109
00:07:19,510 --> 00:07:23,910
Patricia DiMango, offered him a plea to
two misdemeanors, and freedom

110
00:07:23,930 --> 00:07:28,090
that day. If you want that, she
said, I will do that today.

111
00:07:28,110 --> 00:07:30,530
He said: I'm all right.

112
00:07:30,550 --> 00:07:35,670
I did not do it. Other prisoners told
him that in his place, they'd

113
00:07:35,690 --> 00:07:37,580
have said they did it and gone home.

114
00:07:40,220 --> 00:07:45,180
On May the twenty-ninth, 2013, his
thirty-first court date, Judge

115
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:47,480
DiMango told him the
prosecution couldn't go ahead.

116
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:51,870
The man who had accused him had gone
back to Mexico, and without him,

117
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:54,430
prosecutors said, they
couldn't prove the case.

118
00:07:54,960 --> 00:07:58,550
Browder was released the next day,
after about eleven hundred days.

119
00:07:58,1000 --> 00:08:00,050
The charges were dismissed.

120
00:08:00,740 --> 00:08:01,710
He was never tried.

121
00:08:04,190 --> 00:08:09,370
To put that in proportion: in 2013, the
average teenager held on Rikers

122
00:08:09,390 --> 00:08:11,220
stayed about seventy-five days.

123
00:08:11,690 --> 00:08:13,780
He stayed about fifteen times as long.

124
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:17,710
So, the second weight.

125
00:08:18,320 --> 00:08:20,610
The law promised a trial
within six months.

126
00:08:21,240 --> 00:08:23,230
On paper, the clock barely moved.

127
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:26,010
In real life, he waited three years.

128
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:29,830
And all along, the way out was
to say he was guilty.

129
00:08:36,156 --> 00:08:40,136
Rikers Island is four hundred acres in
the East River, with ten jails

130
00:08:40,156 --> 00:08:44,536
on it. In 2010, New York prosecuted
sixteen-year-olds as adults, and

131
00:08:44,556 --> 00:08:48,356
held them there. Browder was sent to
the jail for adolescent boys,

132
00:08:48,376 --> 00:08:50,926
some six hundred of them.

133
00:08:52,326 --> 00:08:56,886
In 2014, after investigating the years he
was there, federal prosecutors

134
00:08:56,906 --> 00:09:00,646
found a pattern of unconstitutional
treatment of the teenagers on

135
00:09:00,666 --> 00:09:03,556
Rikers, and a deep-seated
culture of violence.

136
00:09:03,966 --> 00:09:08,126
Staff used force, they found, not as a
last resort, but to control

137
00:09:08,146 --> 00:09:09,836
the boys and to punish them.

138
00:09:12,456 --> 00:09:17,276
Of the seven hundred and five boys
held there on one day in October

139
00:09:17,296 --> 00:09:20,546
2012, forty-four percent had been
subjected to force by staff at least

140
00:09:21,256 --> 00:09:24,266
once. The investigators' expert said he
had never seen a higher rate.

141
00:09:26,926 --> 00:09:31,646
Punishment meant solitary confinement:
a cell six feet by eight, for

142
00:09:31,666 --> 00:09:36,156
twenty-three hours a day, with an hour
of recreation in a cage outside.

143
00:09:37,066 --> 00:09:41,706
On any given day in 2013, between
fifteen and twenty-five percent

144
00:09:41,726 --> 00:09:43,276
of the teenagers were in it.

145
00:09:43,986 --> 00:09:47,546
On one day that July, nearly three
quarters of those in it had been

146
00:09:47,566 --> 00:09:49,256
diagnosed with a mental illness.

147
00:09:50,716 --> 00:09:52,866
Browder spent much of his
three years there.

148
00:09:53,356 --> 00:09:55,716
The department never released a count.

149
00:09:55,736 --> 00:09:58,496
His own estimate was seven or
eight hundred days.

150
00:09:58,516 --> 00:10:00,396
The reporter put it at
about two years.

151
00:10:00,416 --> 00:10:04,556
The first time, he said, was after a
fight that started with a thrown

152
00:10:04,576 --> 00:10:05,286
sneaker.

153
00:10:07,686 --> 00:10:10,746
He told the reporter about being
handcuffed and searched every time

154
00:10:10,766 --> 00:10:14,826
he left the cell, about one six-minute
phone call a day, about being

155
00:10:14,846 --> 00:10:19,576
hungry. He described officers beating
a line of boys after a fight,

156
00:10:19,906 --> 00:10:23,736
and being beaten himself, by
officers and by other inmates.

157
00:10:24,466 --> 00:10:26,556
The department didn't respond
to those allegations.

158
00:10:26,986 --> 00:10:31,636
In 2015, surveillance video of two of
those beatings was published.

159
00:10:32,506 --> 00:10:33,496
We won't show it.

160
00:10:35,936 --> 00:10:39,536
He tried to take his own life while
he was in solitary, and again

161
00:10:39,556 --> 00:10:40,946
after he came home.

162
00:10:41,936 --> 00:10:45,246
In 2014 he told the reporter:
I'm not all right.

163
00:10:45,576 --> 00:10:48,346
I'm messed up. I'm mentally
scarred right now.

164
00:10:51,746 --> 00:10:56,186
On May the thirtieth, 2013, he walked
out of Rikers and took a bus

165
00:10:56,206 --> 00:10:57,336
and two subways home.

166
00:10:57,786 --> 00:11:02,256
He had missed his junior year of high
school, his senior year, graduation

167
00:11:02,966 --> 00:11:03,476
and the prom.

168
00:11:06,016 --> 00:11:10,436
He passed his high school equivalency
exam on the first try, and started

169
00:11:10,456 --> 00:11:12,066
at Bronx Community College.

170
00:11:12,856 --> 00:11:17,586
In 2014 he decided to tell his story,
because, the reporter wrote,

171
00:11:18,076 --> 00:11:21,616
he wanted the public to know what he'd
been through, so that nobody

172
00:11:21,636 --> 00:11:23,326
else would have to go through it.

173
00:11:23,836 --> 00:11:28,326
He said: in my mind right now, I
feel like I'm still in jail.

174
00:11:31,086 --> 00:11:32,656
So, the third weight.

175
00:11:33,586 --> 00:11:37,266
Waiting for trial meant waiting on
Rikers: in a jail that federal

176
00:11:37,286 --> 00:11:41,836
investigators found was violent toward
the very boys it held, and,

177
00:11:41,966 --> 00:11:45,416
by his account, about two
years alone in a cell.

178
00:11:46,186 --> 00:11:48,056
He was never convicted of anything.

179
00:11:54,663 --> 00:11:57,093
His story was published in
the fall of 2014.

180
00:11:57,883 --> 00:12:01,153
That December, the city ended solitary
confinement for adolescents.

181
00:12:02,043 --> 00:12:06,183
In January 2015, the city's Board of
Correction voted to end it for

182
00:12:06,203 --> 00:12:07,773
everyone twenty-one and under.

183
00:12:09,923 --> 00:12:13,953
On June the sixth, 2015, Kalief
Browder died by suicide.

184
00:12:14,583 --> 00:12:18,203
He was twenty-two. His mother,
Venida, died the next year.

185
00:12:18,223 --> 00:12:22,843
In 2019, New York City settled the
family's lawsuit for three point

186
00:12:22,863 --> 00:12:24,113
three million dollars.

187
00:12:24,623 --> 00:12:28,603
If you're struggling yourself, help is
out there, and it works for

188
00:12:28,623 --> 00:12:31,973
most people: there's a number on
screen and in the description.

189
00:12:36,373 --> 00:12:38,883
The law has long said that
this shouldn't happen.

190
00:12:39,653 --> 00:12:44,333
In 1951, the Supreme Court wrote that
freedom before trial lets the

191
00:12:44,353 --> 00:12:48,103
accused prepare a defense, and prevents
punishment before conviction.

192
00:12:48,753 --> 00:12:52,733
Unless that right is preserved, it
said, the presumption of innocence

193
00:12:52,753 --> 00:12:54,063
would lose its meaning.

194
00:12:54,553 --> 00:12:57,223
Bail is meant to make sure you
come back to court.

195
00:12:57,833 --> 00:13:00,783
Set higher than that
requires, it's excessive.

196
00:13:03,623 --> 00:13:07,883
But in 1987, the Court also said a
person can be held without bail

197
00:13:07,903 --> 00:13:12,363
for being dangerous, if the government
proves at a hearing, by clear

198
00:13:12,383 --> 00:13:16,063
and convincing evidence, that no
conditions of release would keep

199
00:13:16,083 --> 00:13:21,543
people safe. In our society, it wrote,
liberty is the norm, and detention

200
00:13:21,563 --> 00:13:24,633
before trial is the carefully
limited exception.

201
00:13:25,363 --> 00:13:29,463
Justice Marshall, dissenting, wrote
that the shortcuts we take with

202
00:13:29,483 --> 00:13:33,983
those we believe guilty injure only
the wrongfully accused, and, in

203
00:13:34,003 --> 00:13:35,173
the end, ourselves.

204
00:13:38,173 --> 00:13:40,083
New York has changed a lot since.

205
00:13:40,893 --> 00:13:44,523
From 2018, no one under eighteen
has been held on Rikers.

206
00:13:45,233 --> 00:13:50,253
In 2019, the state ended cash bail for
most misdemeanors and nonviolent

207
00:13:50,273 --> 00:13:53,753
felonies, and said prosecutors can't
call themselves ready for trial

208
00:13:53,773 --> 00:13:56,733
until they've handed over
their evidence.

209
00:13:56,753 --> 00:14:00,233
Since then, lawmakers have three times
given judges back some power

210
00:14:00,253 --> 00:14:05,113
to set bail, including for felonies
charged while someone is on probation.

211
00:14:05,133 --> 00:14:07,983
A sixteen-year-old charged today would
not be sent to Rikers at all.

212
00:14:09,203 --> 00:14:10,533
One thing New York didn't change.

213
00:14:11,243 --> 00:14:14,433
Its judges may set bail only to make
sure people come back to court.

214
00:14:14,803 --> 00:14:16,753
They can't weigh whether
someone is dangerous.

215
00:14:17,623 --> 00:14:21,563
A Bronx judge wrote in 2023 that New
York is the only state where

216
00:14:21,583 --> 00:14:25,263
that's so. Prosecutors have asked to be
allowed to argue danger since

217
00:14:25,283 --> 00:14:26,113
2019.

218
00:14:28,693 --> 00:14:30,543
And being held changes
the case itself.

219
00:14:31,593 --> 00:14:35,013
In New York City, economists comparing
judges who set bail more or

220
00:14:35,033 --> 00:14:38,413
less often found that being held
raised a felony defendant's chance

221
00:14:38,433 --> 00:14:41,103
of conviction by thirteen
percentage points.

222
00:14:41,813 --> 00:14:45,913
In Philadelphia and Miami, another
team found much the same, mostly

223
00:14:45,933 --> 00:14:47,063
through guilty pleas.

224
00:14:47,753 --> 00:14:49,123
Browder refused to plead.

225
00:14:49,573 --> 00:14:50,583
Most people don't.

226
00:14:52,603 --> 00:14:56,003
When New York stopped setting bail in
most cases, the people released

227
00:14:56,023 --> 00:15:00,523
were re-arrested less, not more: in the
city, forty-four percent within

228
00:15:00,543 --> 00:15:02,733
two years, against fifty before.

229
00:15:03,403 --> 00:15:07,673
But not everyone. For people with
substantial recent criminal records,

230
00:15:07,903 --> 00:15:11,863
release led to more re-arrests, and so
it did for the kinds of cases

231
00:15:11,883 --> 00:15:14,733
lawmakers later made
eligible for bail again.

232
00:15:17,273 --> 00:15:19,043
England starts from the other end.

233
00:15:19,453 --> 00:15:23,813
By law, a defendant shall be granted
bail, unless, among a few other

234
00:15:23,833 --> 00:15:27,223
exceptions, there are substantial
grounds to believe they'd run, commit

235
00:15:27,493 --> 00:15:29,613
an offense, or interfere
with witnesses.

236
00:15:29,633 --> 00:15:34,253
Danger is a reason to hold
someone, and it's said out loud.

237
00:15:34,273 --> 00:15:37,453
Release doesn't depend on
posting a cash bond.

238
00:15:37,473 --> 00:15:42,393
And England's clock counts days in
custody, not promises to be ready:

239
00:15:42,413 --> 00:15:46,953
a hundred and eighty-two before a
trial in the Crown Court, unless

240
00:15:46,973 --> 00:15:48,203
a court extends it.

241
00:15:50,503 --> 00:15:51,853
So, the fourth weight.

242
00:15:52,323 --> 00:15:56,003
The Supreme Court calls detention
before trial the carefully limited

243
00:15:56,023 --> 00:16:00,963
exception. Yet two in three people in
America's local jails haven't

244
00:16:00,983 --> 00:16:04,653
been convicted. Being held makes a
conviction more likely.

245
00:16:05,043 --> 00:16:09,313
And where New York City released more
people, re-arrests mostly fell,

246
00:16:09,743 --> 00:16:12,213
except among people with
serious recent records.

247
00:16:18,486 --> 00:16:22,436
First, the strongest case for
holding people before trial.

248
00:16:23,186 --> 00:16:27,106
Some people charged with serious crimes
will hurt someone if they're

249
00:16:27,126 --> 00:16:31,986
released, and the cost of a wrong
release falls on a victim, not a

250
00:16:32,006 --> 00:16:36,746
statistic. The Supreme Court allows
detention for danger, after a

251
00:16:36,766 --> 00:16:42,016
hearing. While someone is held, they
can't commit new crimes or skip

252
00:16:42,286 --> 00:16:45,786
court, and the best New York study
found that releasing people with

253
00:16:45,806 --> 00:16:49,136
serious recent records led
to more re-arrests.

254
00:16:49,726 --> 00:16:52,516
And Browder's case isn't
really about bail.

255
00:16:53,386 --> 00:16:55,476
Ending cash bail wouldn't
have freed him.

256
00:16:56,046 --> 00:16:57,976
A probation hold kept him in.

257
00:16:58,606 --> 00:17:00,256
What failed was the delay.

258
00:17:01,856 --> 00:17:03,766
Now the strongest case against.

259
00:17:04,576 --> 00:17:08,436
A person who hasn't been convicted is
presumed innocent, and every

260
00:17:08,456 --> 00:17:12,096
day in jail is punishment
without a verdict.

261
00:17:12,116 --> 00:17:15,356
Money bail doesn't sort the
dangerous from the safe.

262
00:17:15,376 --> 00:17:18,696
It sorts people who can pay
from people who can't.

263
00:17:18,716 --> 00:17:22,526
Being held makes a guilty plea more
likely, whether or not you did

264
00:17:23,156 --> 00:17:28,156
it. Where New York City released more
people, re-arrests mostly fell.

265
00:17:28,176 --> 00:17:31,516
And for a sixteen-year-old in New
York, being held meant a jail the

266
00:17:31,536 --> 00:17:34,076
federal government found had a
culture of violence.

267
00:17:34,096 --> 00:17:39,706
If someone is truly dangerous, a judge
should say so, at a hearing,

268
00:17:40,296 --> 00:17:43,896
and hold them for that reason, not
because of what's in their bank

269
00:17:43,916 --> 00:17:44,426
account.

270
00:17:45,986 --> 00:17:46,876
Here's our verdict.

271
00:17:47,786 --> 00:17:51,676
On the clock: no one should wait
years in jail for a trial.

272
00:17:52,406 --> 00:17:56,996
Time held before trial should have a
hard limit, counted in days,

273
00:17:57,586 --> 00:18:01,686
not in excuses, that only a judge can
extend, for a stated reason.

274
00:18:01,706 --> 00:18:05,466
Our confidence is eighty-five percent.

275
00:18:05,486 --> 00:18:09,786
What would change our mind: evidence
that hard limits mostly free

276
00:18:09,806 --> 00:18:14,266
people who go on to seriously hurt
someone, or rush trials until they're

277
00:18:14,286 --> 00:18:14,796
unfair.

278
00:18:18,956 --> 00:18:23,076
On money bail: not being able to pay
should never be the reason someone

279
00:18:23,096 --> 00:18:27,576
is held. If a judge thinks someone is
too dangerous, or too likely

280
00:18:27,596 --> 00:18:32,706
to run, they should hold them openly,
after a hearing, for that reason.

281
00:18:33,356 --> 00:18:37,746
That means letting judges weigh danger,
which New York still doesn't.

282
00:18:38,116 --> 00:18:40,356
Our confidence is eighty percent.

283
00:18:40,376 --> 00:18:44,356
What would change our mind: evidence
that hearing-based systems end

284
00:18:44,376 --> 00:18:48,606
up holding the same poor defendants,
for as long as money bail did.

285
00:18:52,426 --> 00:18:56,316
And on Kalief Browder: he should never
have spent three years on Rikers.

286
00:18:57,006 --> 00:18:58,076
Money put him there.

287
00:18:58,846 --> 00:19:02,666
An automatic probation hold and a
clock that never ran out kept him

288
00:19:02,686 --> 00:19:06,076
there. Our confidence is
eighty-five percent.

289
00:19:06,406 --> 00:19:10,586
What would change our mind: the sealed
court file showing that most

290
00:19:10,606 --> 00:19:15,106
of the delay came from his own side,
or evidence beyond one man's

291
00:19:15,126 --> 00:19:17,536
account that the case
against him was strong.

292
00:19:21,556 --> 00:19:22,466
That's our verdict.

293
00:19:23,096 --> 00:19:26,006
It's open, which means it can change.

294
00:19:26,616 --> 00:19:29,646
If you think we got it wrong, make
your case in the comments.

295
00:19:30,136 --> 00:19:32,946
We'll answer the strongest one
in the next episode.

296
00:19:36,396 --> 00:19:39,626
Next case: Texas, 1991.

297
00:19:40,776 --> 00:19:42,926
A house fire kills three
young sisters.

298
00:19:43,556 --> 00:19:48,206
Investigators call it arson, and their
father, Cameron Todd Willingham,

299
00:19:48,556 --> 00:19:49,926
is sentenced to death.

300
00:19:50,736 --> 00:19:55,096
Four days before his execution in
2004, a fire scientist tells the

301
00:19:55,116 --> 00:19:58,356
governor's office and the parole
board the arson evidence doesn't

302
00:19:58,376 --> 00:20:00,686
hold up. Texas executes him.

303
00:20:01,656 --> 00:20:04,426
Independent experts have since
rejected the arson findings.

304
00:20:05,236 --> 00:20:06,806
His conviction still stands.

305
00:20:07,676 --> 00:20:10,546
Should a state execute someone when
the evidence is disputed?
